Open-end funds may **not**:
- buy securities on **margin**;
- participate in a **joint trading account**;
- **sell short**;
- own more than **3%** of another investment company's voting securities.

The one thing they *must* do is **issue common stock** — that is the ownership interest.

Side note: because mutual fund shares are a continuous new issue, a broker-dealer can't lend against them at purchase — but may extend margin credit once the customer has owned them **more than 30 days**.

A **majority vote of outstanding shares** is required for **fundamental changes**: switching open-end↔closed-end or diversified↔nondiversified, changing the investment objective or any fundamental policy, or ceasing to be an investment company. Shareholders also approve the **investment adviser** and **principal underwriter** contracts, which must:
- be **in writing** and spell out all compensation;
- after the first two years, be **renewed annually** by the board or shareholders;
- be terminable **any time, without penalty, on no more than 60 days' written notice**;
- be approved by a majority of **noninterested directors** (so no advisory contract takes effect without the outside directors).

A fund needs a **net worth of at least $100,000** before it may offer shares publicly.
